    Refused classes of options: destination overrides (Redshift \c host and \c port), socket factories and class
    names, proxies, pipes and local sockets, local bind addresses, key and trust stores and certificate files,
    configuration and log files, IAM, identity provider, credential and authentication plugins (also the
    SingleStore clear-text and PAM plugins, <b>PROVISIONAL</b>), the SingleStore pool and JMX options, and options
    that only the drivers' plugins read.

    The SingleStore driver serves a server's request to read a client file (\c LOAD \c DATA \c LOCAL \c INFILE)
    whatever \c allowLocalInfile says, if the SQL text matches its own \c LOAD \c DATA \c LOCAL \c INFILE pattern, so
    every SQL text of a restricted SingleStore connection that matches \c LOAD\\s+(DATA\\s+)?LOCAL\\s+INFILE
    (case-insensitive) is refused: in \c initSql and \c sessionVariables (\c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED) and in statements
    (\c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR, before the driver sees the SQL), where the SQL is checked as given and as the driver
    rewrites its JDBC escapes (<tt>{d LOCAL}</tt> becomes \c LOCAL).  Every option value is screened, because the
    driver sends some of them as SQL while it connects (\c initSql, \c sessionVariables after its own parsing,
    \c vectorTypeOutputFormat), before any statement.  SQL that restricted code gives a SingleStore connection that
    trusted code opened (without a guard) is screened in the same way; that such a connection is not restricted
    otherwise is qore#6142.

    <b>PROVISIONAL / BLOCKED:</b> restricted callers' SQL on a connection that trusted code opened is
    screened only for SingleStore URLs.  With other JDBC drivers (ex: MariaDB or MySQL with \c LOAD \c DATA
    \c LOCAL \c INFILE allowed, H2's \c FILE_READ or \c CREATE \c ALIAS, SQLite or Derby), sandboxed code that is
    handed a trusted connection can read files or run code, exactly as before qore#5786; whether such a connection
    must refuse restricted callers is the decision of qore#6142.

    SingleStore authentication and TLS: \c restrictedAuth applies to the plugins that a server switches to, not to
    the plugin of the server's first handshake, so a server can still choose \c mysql_clear_password; the driver
    sends a clear-text password only over TLS, which is \c verify-full for restricted connections, so it reaches only
    the checked destination.  Without a \c keyStore option the driver presents the JVM's client key store
    (\c javax.net.ssl.keyStore) to every server, on every TLS handshake (also for reconnects, transaction replay and
    query cancels), so a restricted connection is forced to use an empty PKCS12 key store of the module (the file
    \c empty.p12 in a new directory of the process with a random name, accessible by its owner only), and the system
    property is never read.  The file is checked for every restricted connection and written again atomically if it
    is missing or changed (and its directory created again if it is missing); a file that is removed later makes the
    next TLS handshakes of the open connections fail, closed.  The directory is used again only if it is still the
    one that the process created (the same file key, its owner, and no access for group or others).  Residual risks,
    all local: the process's own user can make that check accept another directory (a reused inode, NTFS creation
    time tunneling), the owner check being the barrier against other users; a reconnect of a connection that was idle
    for long reads the path of its open without checking it again, and the same window exists when a restricted
    connection makes sure of the store, between the check of the directory and the check or write of the file, so if
    a temporary file cleaner removed the directory and another user created the path, the connection reads that
    user's file: the connection can fail, block on a FIFO, or present a client certificate of a key store that the
    other user put there after the check, but the JVM's key cannot leak (keeping the store outside a cleaned /tmp is
    a possible follow-up); a FIFO that the process's own user swaps in between the check and the read blocks the
    connections that are being prepared; and a file system that ignores permissions (vfat, NTFS with a umask) creates
    a new directory for every restricted connection and can refuse a concurrent one.  Certificate validation follows
    the JVM's configuration: with settings such as \c com.sun.security.enableAIAcaIssuers or OCSP and CRL
    distribution point checking, TLS validation fetches URLs from the server's certificate, outside the sandbox;
    these settings are trusted configuration of the JVM.

    <b>Errors:</b> \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED for a refused driver, URL, option, version or destination, and
    \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR for a failed integrity check (a class of the module's name in the driver's class loader, a
    driver that did not use the module's socket factory, a statement with \c LOAD \c DATA \c LOCAL \c INFILE).

    <b>The drivers' jars</b> must be on the classpath of every Program for restricted connections: the jars of a
    \c classpath option, or of the class loader of a sandboxed Program, are not used for them.

    <b>The Java classes</b> of the package \c org.qore.jni.jdbc open sockets to the destinations they hold without a
    check (ex: \c JdbcConnectGuard.forTargets() is public): the package must never be declared sandbox-aware or
    added to an allowlist of Java classes for sandboxed code.  The classes are defined in the bootstrap class loader
    when %Qore starts the JVM, and come from \c qore-jni.jar in the application class loader when the JVM starts
    %Qore.

    @par Example: a sandbox that allows one Redshift cluster
    @code{.py}
%requires jni
%module-cmd(jni) global-add-classpath /opt/jdbc/redshift-jdbc42-2.3.0.jar

SandboxManager sm();
sm.setNetworkDefaultPolicy(False);
sm.addNetworkAllowedHost("redshift.example.com");
sm.addNetworkAllowedPort(5439, QSEC_NET_TCP);
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE);
p.setSandboxManager(sm);
p.parse("sub run() { Datasource ds({\"type\": \"jdbc\", \"user\": \"user\", \"pass\": \"pass\", "
    "\"db\": \"jdbc:redshift://redshift.example.com:5439/dev\"}); printf(\"%y\\n\", ds.selectRows(\"select 1\")); }",
    "tenant");
p.callFunction("run");
    @endcode

    @par Example: a SingleStore host list
    @code{.py}
Datasource ds({"type": "jdbc", "user": "user", "pass": "pass",
    "db": "jdbc:singlestore:sequential://db1.example.com:3306,db2.example.com:3306/app"});
    @endcode
    Every host of the list must pass the sandbox, or the connection is refused before any host is connected to.

    <b>Open questions</b> (design section 11): whether to ship with the thread as the policy source (qore#6142); the
    qore#5747 contract for \c QDBI_METHOD_GET_CONNECT_TARGETS, which the module implements under its \c #ifdef
    (compiled out with today's %Qore); the reconnect rule in Qore-5747 mode; the empty INI file on Windows; exact
    audited versions or ranges; the error codes; caps for \c fetchRingBufferSize, \c maxResultBuffer and
    \c retriesAllDown; the authentication plugins and TLS modes allowed for sandboxed callers; defining the
    SingleStore adapter in the vendor package; the licence of the SingleStore driver (LGPL-2.1) for the tests; and
    whether a trusted connection that sandboxed code uses must refuse restricted callers' SQL for drivers without a
    profile (qore#6142, see above).

    @section jdbc_driver_options jdbc DBI Driver Options

    The jdbc driver supports the following DBI options:
    - \c "classpath": @ref jdbc_option_classpath "sets the classpath" with jar or class files providing the \c jdbc
      driver
    - \c "lob-max-size": @ref jdbc_option_lob_max_size "the most bytes or characters" read from a large object whose
      length the driver does not give
    - \c "numeric-numbers": return received \c SQL_NUMERIC and \c SQL_DECIMAL values as arbitrary-precision numbers
      (Qore number values)
    - \c "optimal-numbers": return received \c SQL_NUMERIC and \c SQL_DECIMAL values as integers if possible, if not
      return them as an arbitrary-precision numbers; this is the default
    - \c "string-numbers": return received \c SQL_NUMERIC and \c SQL_DECIMAL values as strings (for backwards-
      compatibility)
    - \c "url": @ref jdbc_option_url "sets the URL" for the \c jdbc driver in case the database value in the %Qore
      datasource connection string cannot accommodate the \c jdbc URL because of special characters

    @note By default \c optimal-numbers is set on the driver for maximum compatibility with other %Qore DBI drivers

    @subsection jdbc_option_classpath jdbc classpath Option

    The \c classpath option allows for the classpath to be set before the JVM loads the Java \c jdbc driver.  The
    value of this option must be a string and is subject to environment variable substitution before being used.
    The variables are expanded from the environment of the process when the option is applied: when a connection is
    opened (also each new connection of a \c DatasourcePool), or when the option is set on an open connection, not
    when it is set on a closed datasource; the environment is that of the process at that time, and the expanded
    entries are judged by the code that set the option, as below.

    If this option is used, then the \c java.util.ServiceLoader for \c "java.sql.Driver" objects is run and iterated
    after updating the classpath, to ensure that any new drivers are loaded and available to drivers loaded after the
    initial driver is loaded, otherwise the \c java.sql.DriverManager will not see the new drivers as they were not
    present in the classpath during on-demand static initialization).

    The option adds jars to the class loader of a %Qore %Program, so it follows the rule of the \c add-classpath module
    command (see @ref jni_sandbox): sandboxed code (under a sandbox that does not allow uncontrolled APIs) and code with
    \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS or \c PO_NO_FILESYSTEM cannot use it (\c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR), and its entries are checked
    against the filesystem policy of a sandbox that allows uncontrolled APIs (\c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED).

    The option is applied when a connection is opened, but it is judged by the code that set it: whoever configures
    the datasource decides (with a %Qore library that records the code that sets each datasource option, qore#5741).
    The code that sets the option is the code that calls \c Datasource::setOption(), or that creates the
    \c Datasource with the option (also in a connection string), and for every connection of a \c DatasourcePool the
    code that created the pool; a copy keeps it.  So:
    - a datasource whose \c classpath option restricted code set is refused also when trusted code opens it
    - the option of a trusted datasource or pool is not refused when a connection is opened on the thread of
      restricted code; the driver connects for code that a sandbox that does not allow uncontrolled APIs governs only
      as @ref jdbc_sandbox_connections describes (\c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED for any other JDBC driver), so a trusted
      pool that is shared with such sandboxed code opens a new connection for it only with an audited driver whose
      destinations the sandbox allows; the classpath option does not decide which driver such a connection uses: the
      driver is loaded from the classpath of every Program
    - sandboxed code that sets the option on a datasource with an audited driver's URL is refused before any jar is
      loaded (\c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR)
    - the entries are checked against the filesystem policy of the code that set the option, not of the code that
      opens the connection

    @note
    - a configuration (a connection string, an option hash or serialized data) that trusted code turns into a
      datasource is the trusted code's configuration, wherever it came from
    - the jars are added to the class loader of the %Program that the \c Datasource or \c DatasourcePool object
      belongs to (the call context of its methods), not of the code that opens the connection: a trusted
      \c DatasourcePool that opens a new connection for sandboxed code adds the jars of its configuration to the
      class loader of the trusted %Program that created it, and a datasource that sandboxed code created adds them
      to the class loader of the sandboxed %Program also when trusted code opens it (or a copy of it)

    @subsection jdbc_option_lob_max_size jdbc lob-max-size Option

    The \c lob-max-size option is the most bytes (\c BLOB) or characters (\c CLOB, \c NCLOB) read from a large object
    whose length the driver does not give (\c length() is negative); a larger one raises \c JDBC-LOB-SIZE-ERROR, so
    that a misbehaving driver cannot make a read take all the memory.  The default is 2 GiB; the value must be a
    positive integer.

    @subsection jdbc_option_url jdbc url Option

    The \c url option allows for \c jdbc URLs to be used that contain special characters like \c @ and \c : that would
    cause the datasource to fail to parse when used as a database name.

    @par url Option Example
    @verbatim
    Datasource ds("jdbc:user/pass@ignored{url=oracle:thin:@hostname:1521:dbserver,classpath=/usr/lib/oracle/21/client64/lib/ojdbc8.jar}");
    @endverbatim

    @note the initial \c jdbc: in the url option is optional; if missing, then it is added automatically by the %Qore
    jdbc driver when the internal URL string is passed to the internal Java JDBC call.
*/
